\chapter{\code{FixedString}: Compile-Time Strings as Template Parameters}
\label{ch:fixed-string}

\section{The Problem with String Literals as Template Arguments}

Almost every facility in \DSLtk{} needs a name that the compiler knows
about: the tag of a tree node, the text of a pattern, the identifier of a
rewrite rule, the suffix of a literal, the name of a visitor case. In C++17
and earlier there was no way to write such a name as a template argument.
The classical workaround -- an enumeration, a \code{char} array parameter, an
integer constant -- either cannot hold arbitrary text or leaks an encoding
into every call site.

C++20 changed this. A template parameter may now have a \emph{class type},
provided the type is a \emph{structural type}. A structural type is a
literal type whose base classes and non-static data members are all public
and non-mutable, and whose equality is defaulted. \DSLtk{} exploits this
with a type of eleven lines, and that type is the subject of this chapter.

\section{The Definition}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch05-fixed} is \code{dsl::FixedString}, transcribed from
\code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 5918--5928 without alteration.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={\code{dsl::FixedString}, from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 5918--5928, shown as it appears inside \code{namespace dsl}.},label={lst:ch05-fixed}]
template <std::size_t N> struct FixedString
{
  char data[N]{};
  constexpr FixedString (const char (&s)[N]) { std::copy_n (s, N, data); }
  constexpr std::string_view
  view () const
  {
    return { data, N - 1 };
  }
  constexpr bool operator== (const FixedString &) const = default;
};
\end{lstlisting}

There are four members and no more. A public array of \code{N} characters, a
converting constructor from a string literal, a \code{view()} accessor that
exposes the text, and a defaulted equality operator. There is no
\code{operator std::string_view}, no \code{size()}, no \code{npos}, no
\code{operator<}. Everything the library needs, it needs from these four.

\subsection{Why Each Member Is There}

The array \code{char data[N]} is the payload. It is public, so the type
satisfies the structural-type requirement; it is non-mutable, so the type is
usable as a template argument; and it is an array rather than a pointer or a
\code{std::string} because the value must be a compile-time constant.

The constructor \code{constexpr FixedString (const char (&s)[N])} takes a
reference to an array of exactly \code{N} characters. Because the parameter
is a reference to an array, the length of the literal \emph{is} the template
argument: writing \code{FixedString<4>{"abc"}} binds a four-element array,
and writing \code{FixedString<3>{"abc"}} does not compile at all, because
the literal \code{"abc"} is a four-element array. The \code{std::copy_n}
copies all \code{N} characters, the terminating null included, which is why
the constructor is written as a copy rather than as a range copy of the
text.

The accessor \code{view()} returns \code{{data, N-1}}. The \code{-1}
discards the terminator that the constructor copied in, so the returned
\code{std::string_view} has exactly the length of the original literal and
no terminator of its own. The method is \code{constexpr}, so a
\code{FixedString} can be turned into a view in a constant expression.

The operator \code{operator==} is explicitly defaulted. This is not
decoration: a structural type must have usable equality, and the defaulted
operator compares the arrays element by element. Comparing two
\code{FixedString}s of the same length is therefore a compile-time operation
that the compiler can fold away. In C++20 the defaulted \code{operator==}
also yields a rewritten \code{operator!=}, so \code{!=} is available even
though it is not written.

\subsection{How the Rules Are Satisfied}

C++20 permits a class type to be used as a non-type template parameter only
if it is a structural type. \code{FixedString} satisfies every clause of that
requirement.

Its single data member \code{data} is public and not \code{mutable}. The
type is a literal type: it has no virtual functions, no virtual bases and no
reference members; its constructor and its \code{view()} are
\code{constexpr}; and its destructor is trivial. Its equality operator is
defaulted, which is the last clause. The result is that
\code{dsl::FixedString<4>{"abc"}} is a valid template argument, and
\code{dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">} is a valid type.

\subsection{What the Type Deliberately Omits}

The eleven lines of \code{FixedString} are best read as a list of refusals.
There is no \code{operator std::string_view}, so a \code{FixedString}
cannot be passed where a view is expected without an explicit
\code{.view()}. There is no \code{size()}, so the length of the text must
be obtained from the view. There is no \code{npos}, no
\code{find}, no \code{substr}, no \code{empty}, and no comparison operators
other than the defaulted \code{operator==} and the \code{operator!=} that
C++20 synthesises from it.

This austerity is deliberate. A class-type non-type template parameter
must be a structural type, and every member that could be added has to be
considered against that requirement: a member that made the type
non-literal, or that added a base class or a data member with
non-public access, would disqualify it. A \code{std::string} member would
make the type non-literal in C++17 and earlier and is forbidden outright
by the structural-type rules because a \code{std::string} is not a
structural type in its own right.

The result is a type that does one thing -- hold a compile-time string --
and exposes it through exactly one operation. Every use of a
\code{FixedString} in the library goes through \code{view()}, and the
library's own code is a demonstration of how little is needed.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\code{N}}{N} Includes the Terminator}

The single most common source of confusion with \code{FixedString} is the
meaning of \code{N}. It is the size of the \emph{buffer}, which is one more
than the length of the text. \code{"abc"} has three characters and four
bytes, so it produces \code{FixedString<4>}, and \code{sizeof} of that type
is four.

This is not an arbitrary convention. The constructor's parameter is
\code{const char (&s)[N]}, and the length of a string literal's array type
is one more than the number of characters, because the literal includes its
terminator. Matching \code{N} to the array length is what makes the
constructor a single, uniform signature that works for every literal.

The consequence for \code{view()} is that it subtracts one. The text
\code{"abc"} is stored in a four-byte buffer and observed as a
three-character \code{std::string_view}.

Table~\ref{tab:ch05-sizes} collects the arithmetic.

