\section{Two Different Tags Are Two Different Types}\label{sec:ch05-different}

Because \code{N} is part of the type, \code{FixedString<6>} and
\code{FixedString<7>} are unrelated types. They cannot be compared: the
defaulted \code{operator==} of \code{FixedString<6>} takes a
\code{const FixedString<6>&}, and a \code{FixedString<7>} does not convert
to it. Attempting the comparison is a hard error:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Comparing two \code{FixedString}s of different lengths. The diagnostic is at the point of use, and it names both operand types.},label={lst:ch05-cmp}]
error: no match for 'operator==' (operand types are
       'const dsl::FixedString<4>' and 'const dsl::FixedString<5>')
\end{lstlisting}

This is not a limitation to be worked around; it is the property that makes
\code{FixedString} usable as a template argument. Two template arguments
are the same template argument only if they are the same value \emph{and}
the same type. If the type alone were not enough to distinguish them, the
compiler could not tell \code{pattern<"[0-9]+">} from
\code{pattern<"[0-9]+x">}, and the pattern engine -- which is driven entirely
by the template argument -- could not exist.

The price is that you cannot compare tags of different lengths directly. The
next section shows the standard workaround.

\section{The D Port's Alternative}

The structural-type machinery exists because C++20 has no other way to pass
a string as a template argument. The D port does not need it, and the
difference is instructive.

D has template parameters that are ordinary compile-time values, and a
string literal in D \emph{is} such a value: \code{"abc"} is an
\code{immutable(char)[]}`. So the D port writes its tags directly:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The D port's tags. A D string literal is already a compile-time value, so no carrier type is needed.},label={lst:ch05-dtag}]
auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(1), leaf!"num"(2));
\end{lstlisting}

The D module still ships a \code{FixedString} -- \code{struct FixedString(string
text)} at line 135 of \code{DSLtk.d}, with a \code{view()} method and an
\code{enum value = text;} -- but its own module header says that
\code{FixedString} is optional, because D string literals can be used
directly as template arguments. The C++ port has no such option: without
\code{FixedString}, there would be no way to write \code{dsl::leaf<"num">}
at all.

The D \code{FixedString} is also different in kind. It is a runtime struct
holding a \code{string}, with a compile-time \code{enum value} capturing the
text; it is not a template parameter carrier at all. The two types share a
name and little else.

\section{Comparing Two Tags at Compile Time}

Because the two types cannot be compared, a comparison must go through the
views, which are both \code{std::string_view} and therefore comparable
regardless of the original buffer sizes. The idiom is a two-parameter
template, and it is short enough to be worth writing down.

\begin{pseudocode}[Comparing two compile-time tags]
\textbf{function} same\_tag (A, B)\\
\quad \textbf{if} A is a compile-time string and B is a compile-time string then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} the views of A and B are equal\\
\quad \textbf{otherwise}\\
\qquad \textbf{report} \emph{not a tag comparison}
\end{pseudocode}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch05-same} is the implementation, and it works across
different lengths because it compares \code{std::string_view} objects
rather than the buffers.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Comparing two compile-time tags, even when their buffer sizes differ. The function is \code{consteval}, so a comparison is only possible at compile time.},label={lst:ch05-same}]
/// Compile-time equality of two tags, even when their lengths differ.
template <dsl::FixedString A, dsl::FixedString B>
consteval bool
same_tag ()
{
  return A.view () == B.view ();
}
\end{lstlisting}

Used as \code{same_tag<"abc", "abc">()} the answer is \code{true}; used as
\code{same_tag<"abc", "abd">()} or \code{same_tag<"abc", "abcd">()} it is
\code{false}. Note that the two template parameters have \emph{different}
types in the second case -- \code{FixedString<4>} and
\code{FixedString<5>} -- and the function still compiles, because the
comparison is between views.

There is a subtlety in the declaration. Writing
\code{template <dsl::FixedString A, dsl::FixedString B>} gives the two
parameters independent types, each with its own deduced \code{N}. Writing
\code{template <dsl::FixedString S>} instead would give a single type
shared by both parameters, and would force the two arguments to have the
same length.

\section{Tags as Compile-Time Programs}

Two of the facilities in Table~\ref{tab:ch05-nttp} do not merely carry a tag;
they execute a program written in it. This is the deepest use of
\code{FixedString}, and it is worth understanding separately from the
mechanical use as a name.

\subsection{The Pattern Engine}

\code{dsl::pattern<S>} is a class template parameterised by the pattern text.
Its single public entry point is \code{matches(std::string_view)}, a
\code{constexpr} function whose implementation is a recursive matcher over
the pattern's characters. Because the pattern is a template argument rather
than a run-time string, the matcher is a compile-time computation: given a
literal pattern and a literal input, the answer is available in a
\code{static_assert}.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch05-pattern} shows the property in use. The second
assertion is the interesting one: it asks the compiler to decide, at
compile time, that a particular input matches a particular pattern, and the
compiler accepts or rejects the program accordingly.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A pattern evaluated entirely at compile time. Both assertions are decided by the compiler, not at run time.},label={lst:ch05-pattern}]
static_assert (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches ("12345"));
static_assert (!dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches ("12a45"));
\end{lstlisting}

The pattern language itself is a small subset: literals, \code{.}, character
classes with ranges, and the \code{*} and \code{+} quantifiers. Matching is
anchored at both ends, so the whole input must be consumed. Chapter
\ref{ch:pattern-regex} covers the engine in full.

\subsection{The Byte Regular Expression}

\code{dsl::StaticRegex<Source>} goes further. Its template argument is the
text of a regular expression over bytes, and the class contains a
\code{static constexpr auto program} member: a Thompson NFA compiled from
the expression, built entirely in a constant expression. The compiled
program is therefore available to the compiler as a constant, and its
validity is checked by a \code{static_assert} inside the class
definition:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The compile-time NFA of \code{StaticRegex}, shown as it appears inside \code{namespace dsl}. The \code{static_assert} inside the class fires at compile time if the expression does not compile to a valid program.},label={lst:ch05-nfa}]
template <FixedString Source> struct StaticRegex
{
  static constexpr auto program
      = byte_regex_detail::Compiler<Source.view ().size () * 4
                                    + 8>{ Source.view () }
            .compile ();
  static_assert (program.valid, "invalid static byte regular expression");
  // ...
};
\end{lstlisting}

Here the tag is not merely a name but the source of a data structure. The
compiled program is a template-argument-driven constant, and two
\code{StaticRegex} instantiations with different expressions are unrelated
types with unrelated programs.

\section{Lifetimes and Ownership}\label{sec:ch05-lifetimes}

\code{FixedString} owns its text, in the sense that the text lives in the
object's own \code{data} array. Two consequences follow, and both are traps.

First, \code{view()} returns a \code{std::string_view} that refers to the
object's buffer, not to a copy. The view is therefore only valid for as
long as the \code{FixedString} does:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={A dangling view. The temporary \code{FixedString} dies at the end of the declaration, and the view points into freed memory.},label={lst:ch05-dangle}]
auto view = dsl::FixedString<4>{"abc"}.view ();   // dangling: the
                                                  // temporary is gone
\end{lstlisting}

The rule is the familiar one for any view-returning accessor: keep the
owner alive, or copy. In practice this rarely bites, because a
\code{FixedString} used as a template argument is a compile-time constant
and the views derived from it are constants too.

Second, the null-terminator convention is a property of the \emph{source},
not a guarantee of the type. The constructor copies \code{N} characters from
the array it is given. When the array is a string literal, the last
character copied is the terminator, and \code{view()} drops it. When the
array is not a literal -- say a four-character buffer with no terminator --
the same copy happens, and \code{view()} silently drops the fourth
character:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={A non-terminated source array. The tag reads as three characters, because \code{view()} always drops the last byte of the buffer.},label={lst:ch05-noterm}]
char buffer[4] = { 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd' };
dsl::FixedString<4> tag { buffer };
// tag.view () is "abc": the 'd' is copied into the buffer and then
// discarded by the N-1 in view ()
\end{lstlisting}

The type therefore treats the last byte of the buffer as a terminator by
convention. Since the practical use of \code{FixedString} is with literals,
and a literal is always terminated, this is harmless in practice; but it is
worth knowing that the guarantee comes from the caller, not from the type.

