\section{What the Tag Costs at Run Time}

Here is the part that is easy to overstate. A compile-time tag does not
remain a compile-time tag in the data structure it produces. When
\code{dsl::leaf<"num">(42)} runs, it constructs an \code{dsl::ASTNode} whose
member \code{tag_} is a \code{std::string}. The tag has been \emph{copied}
into a run-time string; it is not a reference to a static buffer, and it
does not carry its identity into the type of the node.

The consequence is that \code{dsl::leaf<"a">(1)} and
\code{dsl::leaf<"b">(1)} have exactly the same type, \code{dsl::ASTNode},
and a tree containing them is indistinguishable, at the type level, from
any other tree. The compile-time tag buys you two things and not a third.
It buys you the ability to write the tag in the source without quoting
syntax noise, and it buys you compile-time dispatch wherever the library
dispatches on a tag -- \code{pattern}, \code{StaticRegex},
\code{ast_schema}, \code{on}. It does not buy you a typed tree.

Figure~\ref{fig:ch05-flow} draws the path a tag takes.

