\subsection{A Worked Example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch05-demo} exercises all of the above in one program: the
buffer arithmetic, the compile-time tag comparison, the fact that the tag
survives into the tree as text, and the fact that it does not survive into
the tree's type.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Compile-time tags: buffer arithmetic, compile-time comparison, and the loss of tag identity at run time.},label={lst:ch05-demo}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <type_traits>

/// Compile-time equality of two tags, even when their lengths differ.
template <dsl::FixedString A, dsl::FixedString B>
consteval bool
same_tag ()
{
  return A.view () == B.view ();
}

int main ()
{
  constexpr dsl::FixedString<6> tag { "abcde" };

  static_assert (tag.view () == "abcde");
  static_assert (tag.view ().size () == 5);
  static_assert (sizeof (tag) == 6);

  static_assert (same_tag<"abc", "abc"> ());
  static_assert (!same_tag<"abc", "abd"> ());
  static_assert (!same_tag<"abc", "abcd"> ());

  static_assert (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::value == "[0-9]+");
  static_assert (dsl::StaticRegex<"[a-z]+">::program.valid);

  auto number = dsl::leaf<"num"> (42);
  auto other = dsl::leaf<"other"> (42);

  static_assert (std::is_same_v<decltype (number), dsl::ASTNode>);
  static_assert (std::is_same_v<decltype (number), decltype (other)>);

  std::cout << "tag.view() = " << tag.view () << "\n";
  std::cout << "leaf tag   = " << number.tag () << "\n";
  std::cout << "leaf value = " << number.value () << "\n";
  std::cout << "dump       = " << number.dump () << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch05-demo}.},label={lst:ch05-demo-out}]
tag.view() = abcde
leaf tag   = num
leaf value = 42
dump       = (num 42)
\end{lstlisting}

The two \code{static_assert}s at the end are the interesting ones. The
first says that \code{dsl::leaf<"num">(42)} has type \code{dsl::ASTNode};
the second says that \code{dsl::leaf<"other">(42)} has the same type. The
compile-time tag has done its work at compile time and left no trace in the
type.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\paragraph{Only string literals can be converted.}
The constructor takes \code{const char (&s)[N]}, so the argument must be an
array of \code{const char}. A \code{std::string}, a \code{const char*}
variable, or a \code{std::string_view} cannot bind to it, and none of them
is a constant expression in any case. Every tag in the library must be
spelled as a literal.

\paragraph{There is no concatenation.}
\code{FixedString} has no \code{operator+} and no way to build a new tag
from two existing tags at compile time. A tag that must be composed at
compile time -- say, a node tag derived from a user-supplied prefix -- has
to be written out in full at each use site, or assembled with a constexpr
function that returns a \code{std::array} and then converted, which the
constructor does not accept.

\paragraph{There is no way to change the length of a tag.}
\code{N} is fixed by the type. A tag of one length cannot be copied into a
tag of another length without a conversion that does not exist.

\paragraph{The view is the only accessor.}
\code{FixedString} provides \code{view()} and nothing else. There is no
\code{operator std::string_view}, so \code{std::string_view sv = tag;}
does not compile and you must write \code{tag.view()}. There is no
\code{size()}, so \code{tag.view().size()} is the way to get the length.
There is no \code{npos}, and there is no \code{operator<} or
\code{operator<=>}, so tags cannot be ordered -- only compared for
equality.

\paragraph{Equality is only available between tags of the same length.}
As Section~\ref{sec:ch05-different} showed, this is a compile-time error
rather than a run-time false. The workaround is to compare views, as in
Listing~\ref{lst:ch05-same}.

\paragraph{Each literal length is a distinct type, and there are many of
them.}
Every distinct literal in a program produces a distinct
\code{FixedString} specialisation, and every specialisation is a distinct
type the compiler must instantiate. A program that uses hundreds of distinct
tags instantiates hundreds of small classes. The cost is small -- an
\code{int}-sized object and a defaulted comparison each -- but it is not
zero, and it is one of the reasons the header's compile time is dominated
by the expression templates and the parser combinators rather than by the
tags.

\paragraph{The tag is copied, and the copy owns its memory.}
As Figure~\ref{fig:ch05-flow} showed, the text ends up in a
\code{std::string}. Each node in a tree owns its own copy of its tag.
For a tree of a million nodes with a four-character tag, that is a million
small heap allocations -- although the small-string optimisation usually
keeps them inside the node itself.

\paragraph{A non-terminated source array loses its last byte.}
As Section~\ref{sec:ch05-lifetimes} showed, \code{view()} always discards
the last byte of the buffer. Constructing a \code{FixedString} from a
non-terminated array therefore silently drops that byte. Only string
literals, which are always terminated, are safe inputs.

\paragraph{The view borrows from the owner.}
\code{view()} returns a view into the object's own buffer. A view taken
from a temporary \code{FixedString} dangles. This is the usual
view-semantics contract, and it is the one place where \code{FixedString}
behaves like a run-time type rather than a compile-time one.

\paragraph{The compile-time tag does not survive into the type of the tree.}
If you need a tree whose node types are known to the compiler, the library
does not provide it. The \code{ast_schema} facility (line 10062) is the
closest thing: it describes a tree's shape at compile time and uses it to
generate a matching run-time tree and to check that a visitor covers every
tag. But the tree itself remains an untyped \code{dsl::ASTNode}.

\section{What Comes Next}

The library's smallest type is now open. You know why \code{FixedString}
exists, what the standard requires of it, what it costs, and where it is
used. Chapter~\ref{ch:feature-tags} turns to the mechanism that carries
these tags into a DSL: the feature tag, its nested mixin, and the ways a
feature can inject members -- including operators -- into the class that
derives from \code{dsl::DSL}.
